<h2 id="understanding-direct-communication-protocols" tabindex="-1">Understanding direct communication protocols</h2>
<p>A direct communication protocol defines how two agents exchange messages, negotiate identity, and coordinate tasks without routing through a central server. In distributed AI systems, this matters because centralized brokers create single points of failure, add latency, and limit horizontal scale. Direct protocols let agents find each other, verify trust, and transfer data over point-to-point connections.</p>
<p><a href="https://swarmsignal.net/multi-agent-communication-protocols-guide/" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Direct communication protocols</a> for AI agents in decentralized networks primarily include four leading standards:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>MCP (Model Context Protocol):</strong> A client-server protocol from Anthropic that uses JSON-RPC 2.0. Agents connect to an MCP server that exposes tools and resources. Best for structured tool access and LLM-driven workflows.</li>
<li><strong>A2A (Agent-to-Agent Protocol):</strong> A peer-to-peer protocol from Google that uses HTTP with JSON-RPC. Agents publish Agent Cards and communicate directly. Best for enterprise orchestration and multi-agent task delegation.</li>
<li><strong>ACP (Agent Communication Protocol):</strong> A REST-based protocol focused on synchronous and asynchronous messaging between agents. Useful when you need simple, stateless request-response patterns.</li>
<li><strong>ANP (Agent Network Protocol):</strong> A decentralized, open protocol built for trust-anchored discovery across public and federated agent networks. Best when agents span organizations or untrusted domains.</li>
</ul>
<p>As explored in our <a href="https://pilotprotocol.network/blog/decentralized-communication-protocols-ai-developers">protocols for AI developers</a> guide, no single protocol handles every scenario. <a href="https://getstream.io/blog/ai-agent-protocols/" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Enterprise deployments favor</a> A2A and MCP, while ANP fits open, decentralized contexts. The four are often used together, which we cover in the perspective section.</p>
