<p>For a deeper look at terminology and protocol mechanics, see our <a href="https://pilotprotocol.network/blog/ai-networking-terminology-a2a-mcp-anp-protocols">A2A, MCP, ANP protocols</a> reference.</p>
<h2 id="setting-up-your-environment-and-requirements" tabindex="-1">Setting up your environment and requirements</h2>
<p>Before you write any protocol logic, get your environment aligned. A misconfigured setup is the leading cause of failed agent registration and silent connection drops.</p>
<p><strong>Core requirements:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Python 3.8 or higher (3.11 recommended for async support)</li>
<li>&quot;mcp<code>SDK for Python (Anthropic's official package) or</code>a2a-sdk\` for A2A workflows</li>
<li><code>httpx</code> or <code>aiohttp</code> for async HTTP transport</li>
<li><code>pydantic</code> for message schema validation</li>
<li>A local or virtual network with open ports for your chosen protocol (typically 8000-8080 for dev)</li>
<li>Linux or macOS preferred; Windows requires WSL2 for reliable socket behavior</li>
</ul>
<p><a href="https://towardsdatascience.com/model-context-protocol-mcp-tutorial-build-your-first-mcp-server-in-6-steps" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">MCP uses JSON-RPC 2.0</a> in a client-server model where your agent is the client and the MCP server exposes tools. A2A flips this: every agent runs a small HTTP server and communicates peer-to-peer.</p>
<p><img src="https://csuxjmfbwmkxiegfpljm.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/blog-images/organization-24002/1776512294878_Developer-configures-MCP-server-in-home-office.jpeg" alt="Developer configures MCP server in home office"></p>
<p>For A2A, you need to publish an Agent Card. <a href="https://medium.com/@vidsagar/building-multi-agent-systems-with-googles-a2a-protocol-5dc58d9b645c" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Agent Cards at <code>/.well-known/agent.json</code></a> let other agents discover your agent’s capabilities, address, and authentication requirements automatically. This is the A2A equivalent of a service registry, but decentralized.</p>
